# Lore.Land Collecting
# Cards and palettes, kept across the whole site: who you have met, and how each
# chapter looks once you have finished it.
#>lore_land_surfaces_collecting
#:surface #!mechanic
#:layer #!pragmatics
#:status #!seeded

~<rationale>{
  A reward on lore.land is the chapter itself, a little more its own. Every
  chapter has a palette mixed from a laminated folio, a place with colours and a
  kind of lettering; finishing the chapter in any way keeps a layer of it, and
  the chapter wears what is kept. The three ways to finish are the three
  dimensions a reader lives in a chapter: reading it, carrying the pie through
  it, keeping its wonders. Cards are the people and things of the book, turned
  where they are first met, in the prose or in the lanes, and kept in one deck.
  Nothing is scored: no points, no streaks, no totals that rise for their own
  sake. A kept thing changes how the book looks or what it remembers, and says
  so where it happens.
}

^"palettes"{
  .note: "book/content/world/palettes.json (one per chapter, plus the mime wash) → book/scripts/modules/collection.mjs → book/styles/components/collection.css (chapter) · home/almanac.css (shelf) · home/hub.css (ribbons)"
  sources: "named for a place and mixed by hand from the maker's own account of a laminated folio on spwashi.com, linked, never copied; where no folio fits yet (Two, Five, Seven) from the chapter plate"
  carries: "colour (ground, ink, accent, glow, edge) and type (family, case, style, tracking, weight, and what lettering it comes from)"
  layers: "colour by reading to the end (headings, kicker, drop cap, rules) · type by carrying the pie through the chapter (the title and section headings take the lettering) · light by keeping three of the wonders first met there (a faint wash of ground and glow)"
  mime_wash: "blue-grey over an older page; all three layers at once, by winning any chapter back from Mimes Against WAP"
  wearing: "a chapter wears the layers kept, mixed with the binding, never laid over it; dark bindings take glow and accent instead of ink; the keepsakes panel at the end of the chapter shows the whole palette, kept or not, and a switch to set it aside"
  projected: "the projection spell names it: =palette[pool]{ colour type } (earned, never chosen, so a link cannot lend one) and !plain[palette] (a link may ask for one visit in the binding's own colours)"
  extracted: "the almanac lets a kept palette be carried away as a Spw atom or as CSS tokens (--pal-ground, --pal-ink, --pal-accent, --pal-glow, --pal-edge, --pal-type-*)"
  home: "a kept palette runs along the foot of its chapter plate in the contents, taller the more of it is kept"
}

^"cards"{
  .note: "book/scripts/home/pie-cards.mjs CARDS (everyone in the lanes) + collection.mjs SITE_CARDS (the torch-ants, BoneWAP: the chapters only)"
  turning: "a name crossing the middle of the screen in a chapter turns its card, one notice at a time; meeting it in the lanes turns it too; both keep one shelf (lore.cards.v1, with the game record lore.pie.v1 cards)"
  showing: "the almanac deck (face-down cards say where the book first has them), the game deck (a card met while reading is face-up there), and the keepsakes panel at the end of each chapter (cards first turned there)"
  care: "children are never cards; the figure is never named; the Egg is not explained"
}

%kept{
  .note: "recorded measures, counted and never chosen: %kept{ cards: n palettes: n layers: n } in the projection spell"
}
